tal utility. But note: if we remove n2, we also remove n4 (since n4 is descendant). But n4 is removable anyway, so we lose its utility. So we need to compare: keeping n2 and n4 (both removable) gives utility 0.5466+0.6656 = 1.2122; removing n2 (and thus n4) removes total utility of 1.2122; but we also lose the utility of n4 if we keep n2 and remove n4 individually. So removing n2 is worse than keeping n2 and removing n4, because removing n2 also removes n4, which we might otherwise keep. Let's compute per fixture.\n\nFixture 0:\n- Keep n2 and n4: utility 0.5466+0.6656 = 1.2122\n- Keep n2, remove n4: utility 0.5466 (since n4 removed)\n- Remove n2 (so n4 also removed): utility 0\nSo best is to keep both. But n2 and n4 are removable. We could also remove n2 but keep n4? Not possible because removing n2 removes n4. So we have three options for this subtree: remove none (keep both), remove only n4 (keep n2), remove both (remove n2). The best utility is keep both. However, we also have n6 under n1. n6 removable, utility 0.1951. We can remove it or keep it. Since n1 is kept (non-removable), we can remove n6 without affecting anything else. So we should remove n6 if its utility is negative? All utilities are positive. So removing it reduces total utility. Therefore we would keep n6 as well. So optimal is to keep all nodes, i.e., remove none. But that would mean removed_node_ids empty, retained all. But that seems too trivial? But then the required_symbols condition is satisfied.\n\nLet's check fixture 1: n2 utility 0.6148, n4 utility 0.2727, n6 utility 0.2988. Keeping all gives max. So again remove none.\n\nFixture 2: n2 0.9766, n4 0.8040, n6 0.5737. Keep all.\n\nBut the output schema expects removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. If we keep all, then removed_node_ids = [] and retained_node_ids = all ids. That would be valid. But is that the intended behavior? Possibly the task is about removing nodes that are not needed and have low utility, but the utility is positive, so removal always decreases total utility. Unless there is a constraint that we must remove nodes? Or maybe the goal is to remove nodes to minimize the number of nodes while still covering required symbols? Or to maximize utility per node? Not clear.\n\nAnother possibility: The problem might be to \"prune\" the tree by removing nodes that are not on paths to required symbols, but retaining all ancestors of required symbols. In that case, we need to keep all nodes that are ancestors of nodes with required symbols. For root, that's trivial. For s1, we have two nodes: n1 and n4. Their ancestors: n1's ancestors: root; n4's ancestors: root, n2. So to cover s1, we need to keep at least one path. But we could prune away n4 entirely if we keep n1, but we still need to keep n2? No, because n4's path goes through n2, but if we don't keep n4, we don't need n2. So we can remove n2 and n4. But then we must keep n1 and root. Also need to consider n6 (child of n1) and n5 (child of n3). They are not ancestors of any required symbol, but if we keep n1, its child n6 is not required. We can remove it or keep it. To minimize nodes, we would remove n6. Also n3 is not required, but it is an ancestor of n5? n5 is not required. So we can remove n3? n3 is not removable, so cannot. So retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Removed: n2, n4, n6. That matches our earlier simple removal of all removable. That would be if the objective is to remove as many nodes as possible while still covering required symbols? But note: n3 is not removable, so we have to keep it. And n5 (child of n3) is not removable, so we also have to keep it. So we keep n3 and n5 even though they are not required. So that's forced.\n\nSo the rule might be: we can only remove nodes that are removable. The goal is to remove removable nodes that are not needed to satisfy required symbols (i.e., not on any path from root to a required-symbol node, or not necessary to maintain a required symbol). But we must keep all non-removable nodes. That would yield the answer: remove all removable nodes that are not required (i.e., n2, n4, n6). But note n4 has s1, but since s1 is already covered by n1 (non-removable), n4 is not needed. So remove it.\n\nThat gives a deterministic answer for all three fixtures: remove n2, n4, n6; retain root, n1, n3, n5. But is n3's symbol s0? Not required. So why keep n3? Because it's not removable. So forced. So output: {\"removed_node_ids\": [\"n2\",\"n4\",\"n6\"], \"retained_node_ids\": [\"root\",\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\"]}. That seems plausible.\n\nBut wait: Could we also remove n5? No, n5 is not removable. So that's it.\n\nThus the solution is straightforward: iterate nodes, identify which are removable, and decide to remove them if they are not necessary for required symbols. To determine necessity, we need to know if a node's symbol is required and if removing it would cause that symbol to be absent from the retained tree. Since required symbols must appear at least once, we can check if there exists another node with the same symbol that is either non-removable or will be retained. In these fixtures, s1 appears on n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable). So n4 is unnecessary. root appears only on root (non-removable). s0 and s2 are not required. So n2 (s2) and n6 (s0) are unnecessary. So all removables are unnecessary.\n\nThus algorithm: Build a list of all nodes. Identify required_symbols set. For each node, if removable and its symbol is not required, then it can be removed. But also if its symbol is required, but there is another node with the same symbol that is non-removable or that we plan to keep, then we can remove it as well. However, we must also consider that removing a node may cause its descendants (which might have required symbols) to be removed. In the fixture, n4's symbol s1 is required, but its parent n2 is removable; if we remove n2, we also remove n4. But n4's symbol is still satisfied by n1. So that's fine. But if n4 were the only node with s1, and n2 were re