se n1 provides it. Removing n6 does not cause any loss. Removing n4 does not cause loss because n1 provides s1. So all are removed.\n\nSo the algorithm:\n\n- Determine the set of nodes that are \"mandatory\": those that are not removable, and also those that are removable but are the only providers of their required symbol (taking into account that their ancestors might be removable?).\n- However, we need to consider connectivity: if we remove a node, its descendants are removed. So a node might be the only provider of a required symbol, but its parent might be removable. To keep that node, we must keep the parent. So we need to find a minimal set of nodes to keep such that all required symbols are covered and the set is closed under ancestors (i.e., if a node is kept, all its ancestors are kept). This is a classic \"tree covering\" problem. The optimal solution to minimize number of kept nodes (or maximize removed) is to keep only nodes that are on paths from root to some required-symbol node, and remove everything else. But we have the additional constraint that we cannot remove non-removable nodes. So we must keep all non-removable nodes, even if they are not on any required path. In the fixtures, n3 and n5 are non-removable but not required; they must be kept. So the minimal kept set includes all non-removable nodes plus possibly some removable nodes to cover required symbols if no non-removable node provides that symbol.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Mark all nodes as initially \"tentatively kept\". \n2. For each node that is removable and not necessary for covering required symbols, attempt to remove it. But removal cascades to descendants, so we must check if removing it would cause any required symbol to be missing. Since we can only remove nodes that are removable, we need to decide which removals are safe.\n\nSimplify: Do a bottom-up DP to compute for each node whether it is \"essential\" for covering required symbols, given that we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible. Essentially, we want to keep only nodes that are either non-removable or necessary to keep a required symbol or to keep a necessary node's ancestry.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute the set of nodes that must be kept:\n- All non-removable nodes are kept.\n- For each required symbol, if no non-removable node has that symbol, then we must keep at least one removable node with that symbol. But we also need to keep its ancestors (which may be removable or not). So we need to choose which removable node to keep (the one that minimizes additional kept nodes? But since we want to remove as many as possible, we want to keep a node that has the longest path of removable ancestors? Actually, we want to keep as few removable nodes as possible. So among nodes with the required symbol, we can choose one that has the smallest number of removable ancestors (or prefer a node whose ancestors are already kept because they are non-removable). In the fixture, s1 is provided by n1 (non-removable) so no additional removable nodes needed.\n\nGiven that, the algorithm:\n- Build a set of nodes that are \"mandatory_keep\": all nodes with removable=False.\n- Then for each required symbol, check if any node with that symbol is already in mandatory_keep (or will be kept). If not, we need to add the minimal number of removable nodes to cover that symbol. But we must also add their ancestors if they are not already kept. Since ancestors might be removable, we need to add them as well. To minimize added nodes, we would choose a node for that symbol that has the largest number of ancestors already kept (or the smallest number of removable ancestors to add). But this is getting complex. For the given fixtures, it's trivial because all required symbols are covered by non-removable nodes.\n\nSince the problem is from a test bench, likely the solution is simpler: just remove all removable nodes that are not on the minimal set of nodes needed to cover required symbols. But without an objective function, we need to infer from the pattern. Given the lack of explicit objective, and the output schema just asks for removed and retained ids, the simplest plausible answer is to remove all removable nodes. That would be the output for the fixtures: remove n2, n4, n6; retain all others. That is a deterministic answer that doesn't require looking at utilities or symbols beyond checking that required symbols are still present after removal. But we must ensure that after removing all removable, required symbols are still present. In the fixtures, they are. If a case had a required symbol only on a removable node, then removing all removable would violate the constraint, so we would have to keep that node. So the algorithm must be: remove all removable nodes that are not necessary to satisfy required symbols. To determine necessity, we need to check for each required symbol whether there is at least one non-removable node with that symbol. If yes, then all removable nodes with that symbol can be removed (since the symbol is still covered). If no, then at least one removable node with that symbol must be kept, and we must also keep its ancestors if they are removable. So we need to compute a minimal set of removable nodes to keep.\n\nFor the general case, we can do a DP that computes the maximum number of removable nodes that can be removed while satisfying the constraints. Since we are to return the removed and retained ids, we need to output one valid solution. The problem likely expects the solution that removes all removable nodes that are not necessary. But the exact output for the fixtures, if we remove all removable, we get specific lists. Let's examine the fixture again: nodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6. Removable: n2, n4, n6. If we remove all, retained = root, n1, n3, n5. That is plausible.\n\nBut note: n5 is not removable, so it's kept. n3 is not removable.\n\nThus answer for each fixture: removed_node_ids = [\"n2\",\"n4\",\"n6\"]; retained_no