if any required symbol is missing, we add back the necessary nodes. A practical approach:\n- Start with all nodes as \"initially_removed\" except non-removable (which are always retained).\n- For each required symbol, check if there is a node with that symbol that is either non-removable or currently retained. If not, we need to retain one removable node that has that symbol. Choose any, e.g., the first one found. Then we must also retain all its ancestors (if they are removable) because otherwise the node would be disconnected. So we add that node and its removable ancestors to retained set.\n- After processing all required symbols, we have a set of retained nodes (including all non-removable). Then removed nodes are all nodes not in retained. But note: if we retain a node, we must also retain its ancestors (non-removable are already retained, removable ancestors might have been removed, so we need to add them). This approach ensures connectivity.\n\nBut this is a greedy that may not be minimal in number of retained removable nodes, but it will produce a valid solution. For the fixtures, it will result in: start with retained = {root, n1, n3, n5} (non-removable). For symbol \"root\": already covered by root. For \"s1\": already covered by n1. No additions. So removed = {n2,n4,n6}. That matches.\n\nIf we had a case where s1 only on n4 and n4's parent n2 is removable, and n1 doesn't exist, then start retained = non-removable (say root and maybe others). s1 not covered, so we pick n4, then add n2 (ancestor) if removable. So retained adds n2 and n4. Works.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    root = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is None:\n            root = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    # Initially retained: all non-removable nodes\n    retained = set(nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if not n['removable'])\n    # We need to ensure required symbols are covered.\n    # Build a set of symbols covered by currently retained nodes.\n    covered_symbols = set()\n    for nid in retained:\n        covered_symbols.add(node_map[nid]['symbol'])\n    # For each required symbol not yet covered, we need to add nodes.\n    for sym in required:\n        if sym in covered_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Find a node with this symbol that is removable.\n        candidates = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n['symbol'] == sym and n['removable']]\n        if not candidates:\n            # No node has this symbol at all? Should not happen per constraints.\n            continue\n        # Choose one candidate, e.g., the first.\n        chosen = candidates[0]\n        # Add chosen node and all its removable ancestors to retained.\n        add_nodes = set()\n        current = chosen\n        while current is not None:\n            if current not in retained:\n                add_nodes.add(current)\n            # move to parent\n            parent = node_map[current]['parent']\n            current = parent\n        retained.update(add_nodes)\n        # Update covered_symbols: the chosen node's symbol, plus maybe others from added nodes? Not necessary for correctness, but we recompute later.\n        # Recompute covered symbols after addition? Simpler: after adding, we can continue loop; but to avoid missing other symbols that might be covered by these added nodes, we can recompute covered_symbols.\n        covered_symbols = set(node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in retained)\n    # After ensuring coverage, compute removed set.\n    all_ids = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = all_ids - retained\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": sorted(list(removed)),\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained))\n    }\n```\n\nNote: The problem does not specify ordering, but output as arrays; 